                                       Basic Linux commands

username @ hostname :~ $

username = name of loogged in user
hostname = Machine name
:~ = directory name
$ 0r # = user Types

root user: #
normal user: $

**Command Format**

<command>  <options>  <arguments>

command: perform specific task
options: filters
arguments: specifing input like file/folder


1. sudo su - / sudo -i 
-->  this command is used to login into root account.
syntax: sudo su -

2. pwd : (Present Working Directory)
--> current working directory
syntax: pwd

3. man man : (manual)
--> to open manual
syntax: man man
--> type 'q' for quit

4. ls : (list)
--> to display current directory/folder contents
i] syntax: ls
ii] syntax: ls <folder_name>
--> to displat specific folder/directory contents
eg:  ls /etc

5. mkdir: (make directory)
--> to create directory/folder
i] syntax: mkdir <folder_name>
eg: mkdir demo

--> using mkdir you can create multiplr directories
ii] syntax: mkdir <folder1> <folder2>.....
eg: mkdir folder1  folder2  folder3  folder4


--> nested directory
iii] mkdir -p parent_dir/child_dir
eg: mkdir -p  dev/test


6. clear / ctrl+l
--> to clear screen
syntax: clear

7. rmdir : (remove directory)
--> to remove empty directory
syntax: rmdir <folder_name>
eg: rmdir demo


folder1/fruits
folder2/fruits

ls /fruits

8. rm -rf:
--> is used to delete non empty directory.
syntax: rm -rf <dir_name>
here,
rm stands for Remove
-r stands for Recursive
-f stands for forecfully

-r tells rm to recursively delete the directory and all its sub contents.
"resursively means that the command will operate on a directory and all its sub directories and contents"
eg: rm -rf  demo

9. cd : Change Directory
--> used to move between directories
syntax: cd <dir_name>
eg: cd demo

10. touch :
--> used to create empty file
syntax: touch <file_name>
eg: touch demo.txt

11. cat :
--> cat command is used to create file and write contents to it.
syntax: cat > <file_name>
eg: cat > test

"type file contents and then use ctrl+d to save and exit"

12. mv : (move)
--> mv command has two use cases:
i] renaming a file:
syntax: mv <file_old_name> <file_new_name>
eg: mv demo dev

ii] move a file:
syntax: mv <source_nm> <destination>
eg: mv  demo  folder

13. cp : (copy)
-->this command is used to copy file
syntax:  cp  <source_file>  <destination>
eg:  cp  demo folder

14. cp -r :
--> this command is used to copy dircetory
syntax: cp -r  <folder_nm>  <destination>
eg: cp -r  folder  new_folder

15. head:
--> used to display first few line (10 lines) of file
syntax:  head  <file_name>
eg: head  demo.txt

16. tail:
--> used to display last few lines (10 lines) of a file.
syntax: tail <file_name>
eg: tail demo.txt

17. wc : (word count)
--> it is used to find out no of lines, word count, character count.
syntax: wc <file_name>
eg: wc file.txt

18. rm : remove
--> to remove files
syntax: rm <file_name>
eg: rm demo.txt

19. sort : 
--> used to sorf a file
syntax: sort <file_name>
eg: sort file.txt

20. uniq:
--> used to delete all adjancent duplicate lines.
syntax: uniq <file_name>
eg: uniq file.txt

21. pipe (|)
--> a pipe is used to send output of one command as a input to another command
syntax: command1 | command2
eg: sort file.txt | uniq

22. grep: 
--> grep is used to search for a specific word or pattern inside files.
syntax: grep <file_name>
eg: grep demo.txt







